{
 "id": "072-ninemin-oruntulu-kilimi",
 "lang": "en",
 "title": "Grandma's Patterned Kilim",
 "theme": "Patterns (AB-AB) and coping with anger",
 "blurb": "Can and his grandma lay out the design for the new kilim rug on the floor: star, triangle, star, triangle. When Cinnamon the cat grabs two pieces and runs off, Can gets very angry; but he takes a breath, finds the pieces and reads the pattern to fill the gaps in the right order.",
 "voiceNames": {
  "can": "Can",
  "nine": "Grandma (Can's grandma)",
  "tarcin": "Cinnamon (cat)"
 },
 "pages": [
  {
   "text": "Grandma laid out the pattern for the new kilim rug on the carpet. Felt stars, felt triangles. “The star is my village's sky, the triangle is my village's mountains,” she said. Can read it with his finger: “Star, triangle, star, triangle…” Always the same order! Grandma laughed: “That's called a pattern.”",
   "hits": {
    "nine": {
     "say": "In our village, kilims are always woven like this."
    },
    "can": {
     "say": "Star, triangle, star, triangle!"
    },
    "tarcin": {
     "say": "Purrr… purrr…"
    }
   },
   "mech": {
    "done": "Star, triangle! Always in the same order.",
    "msg": "You read the pattern!"
   },
   "hint": "Read from left to right!",
   "ask": "What always comes after the star? Is there something with a pattern in your home?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Cinnamon the cat woke up. Hop! She jumped onto the pattern. She grabbed a triangle and a star and ran off, pit pat. Two gaps were left in the pattern. Can was so angry. His cheeks went hot, his hands made fists. His heart went thump, thump. “Cinnamon! You ruined our pattern!”",
   "hits": {
    "can": {
     "say": "Cinnamon! You ruined our pattern!"
    },
    "tarcin": {
     "say": "Meow! Catch me!"
    },
    "nine": {
     "say": "Oh! Two pieces have run away!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Touch Can!",
   "ask": "How is Can feeling? What happens in your body when you get angry?",
   "cues": [
    {
     "w": 45,
     "fx": "kalp"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Grandma sat beside Can. “First, let's take a breath.” In through the nose, out through the mouth: ohhh. His heart slowed, his fists opened. “Cinnamon thought it was a game,” said Can. “But where did she take the pieces?”",
   "hits": {
    "nine": {
     "say": "In through the nose… out through the mouth… ohhh."
    },
    "can": {
     "say": "Ohhh… I feel better now."
    }
   },
   "predict": {
    "q": "Where do you think Cinnamon took the pieces?",
    "res": "Could be either! Let's look.",
    "opts": [
     {
      "l": "Under the sofa"
     },
     {
      "l": "Behind the cushion"
     }
    ]
   },
   "hint": "Breathe with Can!",
   "ask": "How did Can calm down? Let's take a breath together."
  },
  {
   "text": "Can searched on his knees. Under the sofa, a yellow point shone: the star! Behind the blue cushion, a red corner: the triangle! Cinnamon swished her tail. “Meow! Is hide-and-seek over?”",
   "hits": {
    "can": {
     "say": "Where are you, pieces?"
    },
    "tarcin": {
     "say": "Meow! Is hide-and-seek over?"
    }
   },
   "mech": {
    "done": "You found them! The star and the triangle are both here.",
    "msg": "You found them both!"
   },
   "hint": "Find the hidden pieces!",
   "ask": "Where were the pieces hiding? Where do you look when you lose something?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Can put the pieces next to the pattern. But which one goes where? He read it from the start with his finger: “Star, triangle, star…” What comes next? Grandma smiled: “No hurry. As you read it, the pattern speaks.”",
   "hits": {
    "can": {
     "say": "Star, triangle, star… what comes next?"
    },
    "nine": {
     "say": "No hurry. The pattern speaks."
    }
   },
   "mech": {
    "done": "Star, triangle, star, triangle! The pattern is complete!",
    "msg": "The gaps are filled!"
   },
   "hint": "What comes next?",
   "ask": "Which piece should go in the gaps? How did you know?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Both gaps were filled. Grandma would weave the pattern on her loom, row by row, just like her own grandma. Can made his own pattern too: red cushion, blue cushion, red cushion, blue cushion. Cinnamon curled up right in the middle. Can giggled: “Red, blue, Cinnamon, red, blue!” Can felt warm inside.",
   "hits": {
    "nine": {
     "say": "How lovely, Can!"
    },
    "can": {
     "say": "Red, blue, Cinnamon, red, blue!"
    },
    "tarcin": {
     "say": "Purrr… I'm a pattern too!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Read the cushions in order!",
   "ask": "Did Cinnamon fit the pattern? Where would you put Cinnamon?",
   "mech": {
    "done": "Red, blue, red, blue!",
    "msg": "You read the cushions!"
   }
  }
 ],
 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "MAB.4 — Making inferences about mathematical facts, events and objects (MAB.4.b: Compares objects, facts and events): compares the star and triangle motifs; notices that the unit “star, triangle” repeats, and decides which motif goes in the gap by looking at the pattern.",
   "OB5 Cultural Literacy — notices that kilim motifs repeat, that Grandma gives the motifs meanings linked to her village (star: the village sky, triangle: the mountains), and that kilims are woven from one generation to the next; connects this with patterned things in their own home.",
   "E3.2 Focus — reads the pattern from the start, following it from left to right with a finger; finds the next motif without rushing.",
   "SDB1.2 Self-regulation — like Can, names their anger (cheeks go hot, hands make fists, heart goes thump, thump), calms down by breathing and returns to solving the problem.",
   "D7 Aesthetics — notices that the regular repetition of colours and shapes makes a beautiful design, and repairs the broken pattern with care."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Show a patterned piece of fabric, a kilim, a headscarf or a sock from home or the classroom: “Is there something here that repeats?”",
   "Lay yellow stars and red triangles cut from paper on the rug and prepare the story's pattern together."
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 1, touch the motifs in order from left to right while reading them together with a rhythm: “star, tri-an-gle…”",
   "On page 2, pause: “How is Can feeling? How can you tell from his body?” Point to his cheeks and his fists. Explain that the faint places with question marks are the gaps: “The places with question marks are gaps.”",
   "On page 3, do Grandma's breath together: in through the nose, out with an “ohhh.” Accept both answers in the prediction.",
   "On page 5, before matching, have the child read the pattern from the start with a finger; ask “What comes next?”, don't give the answer, let them find it.",
   "On page 6, read the cushions in order; laugh when Cinnamon gets in the middle and ask: “Did Cinnamon fit the pattern?”"
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "Which two shapes took turns repeating in Grandma's pattern?",
   "What did Can do when he got angry, and how did he calm down?",
   "Why might Cinnamon have taken the pieces? What could we say instead of getting angry?"
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "Class Kilim: Pattern Strip",
   "sure": "20 min",
   "materyal": "A long strip of kraft paper, stars and triangles cut from coloured card (or bottle tops/buttons in two colours), glue, a real kilim rug or a photo of one",
   "adim": [
    "Look closely at the kilim or its photo; the children point out the repeating motifs, and the teacher asks “What does this motif look like?”",
    "The teacher lays “star, triangle, star, triangle” at the start of the strip; the children take turns to continue the pattern, and after each addition everyone reads it from the start together.",
    "The teacher secretly takes two motifs away (the Cinnamon game); the children work out which goes in the gaps by reading the pattern. Any child who wants to can build their own two-colour pattern."
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "Which shapes were in Grandma's pattern?",
   "How did Can calm down when he was angry?",
   "Can you find a repeating pattern in our home?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "Pattern hunt at home and a sock line",
   "d": "Look together at patterned things at home (curtains, rugs, plates, socks) and trace the repeating shape with a finger. Then build a “one of this, one of that” pattern with socks in two colours, spoons and forks, or pasta; hide one piece and ask your child to find the missing one."
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “Grandma's Patterned Kilim.” While laying out the kilim design with his grandma, Can discovered that the star and the triangle repeat in turn, which means it's a pattern. When the cat ran off with two pieces he got very angry; he took a deep breath, calmed down and filled the gaps himself by reading the pattern. At home, you can look together for repeats in the patterns on rugs, headscarves or plates, and when your child gets angry you can say, “Shall we take a breath like Can?”"
 },
